# 11. Adding a New Authenticator

When the user scans a QR code:

```text
QR Code
   ↓
otpauth:// URI
   ↓
Parser
   ↓
Validate Parameters
   ↓
Create Account Object
   ↓
Vault Manager
   ↓
Encrypt Vault
   ↓
Write File
```

Example URI:

```text
otpauth://totp/GitHub:user@example.com?secret=...&issuer=GitHub
```

The secret is extracted locally.

There is no API request.

---

# 12. Saving a New Account

The sequence should be:

```text
User scans QR
      ↓
QR parser
      ↓
TOTP configuration
      ↓
User confirms account
      ↓
Vault unlocked
      ↓
Account added
      ↓
Vault re-encrypted
      ↓
Atomic file replacement
      ↓
Vault locked
```

The application should avoid leaving an unencrypted temporary vault on disk.

---

# 13. Generating OTP Codes

When the user opens an account:

```text
Encrypted Vault
      ↓
Authenticate user
      ↓
Decrypt vault in memory
      ↓
Read TOTP secret
      ↓
Current system time
      ↓
TOTP algorithm
      ↓
Generate OTP
      ↓
Display OTP
```

Example:

```text
GitHub
●●● ●●●

Expires in 18 seconds
```

The generated OTP does not need to be sent anywhere.

---

# 14. Memory Security

The application should minimize the lifetime of sensitive information in memory.

When the vault is locked:

```text
Vault key
TOTP secrets
Decrypted account database
```

should no longer remain unnecessarily available in application memory.

Where the programming environment allows it, sensitive buffers should be cleared after use.

The application should also avoid:

* Logging secrets
* Debug-printing secrets
* Crash-reporting secrets
* Clipboard retention longer than necessary
* Writing secrets into temporary files

---

# 15. Backup Architecture

The backup system is one of the most important components.

The user selects:

**Settings → Export Backup**

Then:

```text
Encrypted Vault
      ↓
Backup Manager
      ↓
Generate backup package
      ↓
Encrypt / authenticate
      ↓
.aax backup
      ↓
User chooses storage
```

The application itself does not upload the backup.

The user can manually store it on:

* USB
* External storage
* Personal cloud storage
* Another computer
* Another phone

The backup remains encrypted.

---

# 16. Backup File

Example:

```text
Anoneurx-Authenticator-Backup.aax
```

Logical structure:

```text
AAX
├── Magic
├── Version
├── KDF Parameters
├── Salt
├── Encryption Parameters
├── Nonce
├── Ciphertext
└── Authentication Tag
```

The exact binary format should be formally versioned.

This allows future versions of the application to migrate old backups.

---

# 17. Backup Restoration

On a new device:

```text
Install Anoneurx Authenticator
             ↓
       Import Backup
             ↓
       Select .aax
             ↓
       Enter Password
             ↓
          Verify
             ↓
       Decrypt Vault
             ↓
       Validate Data
             ↓
       Import Accounts
             ↓
       Re-encrypt locally
             ↓
       New Device Vault
```

No Anoneurx server is involved.

---

# 18. Device Migration

Example:

```text
             OLD DEVICE
                 │
                 │
        Export encrypted vault
                 │
                 ▼
          Backup.aax
                 │
        USB / Files / etc.
                 │
                 ▼
             NEW DEVICE
                 │
          Import Backup
                 │
                 ▼
         Enter backup password
                 │
                 ▼
          Restore accounts
```

This makes the application independent of a central Anoneurx account.

---

# 19. No Cloud Synchronization

Anoneurx Authenticator should deliberately **not** implement:

```text
Phone A
   ↓
Anoneurx Cloud
   ↓
Phone B
```

Instead:

```text
Phone A
   ↓
Encrypted Backup
   ↓
User-controlled transfer
   ↓
Phone B
```

This greatly reduces the attack surface.

---

# 20. Offline Network Policy

For the strongest privacy architecture, the application should not require networking at runtime.

Ideally:

```text
Network Permission
        │
        ▼
       NONE
```

The application should not depend on:

* Authentication APIs
* Cloud databases
* Analytics servers
* Remote configuration
* Telemetry
* Advertising SDKs

This also means the application can function in airplane mode.

---

# 21. Threat Model

The architecture should explicitly consider:

### Threat 1 — Cloud database breach

Mitigation:

```text
No cloud database
```

### Threat 2 — Server compromise

Mitigation:

```text
No authentication backend
No secret API
```

### Threat 3 — Network interception

Mitigation:

```text
No network communication required
```

### Threat 4 — Stolen backup

Mitigation:

```text
Strong password
+
KDF
+
Authenticated encryption
```

### Threat 5 — Lost phone

Mitigation:

```text
Encrypted local vault
+
Manual encrypted backup
```

### Threat 6 — Modified backup

Mitigation:

```text
AEAD authentication
```

The application should refuse to import a corrupted or tampered vault.

---

# 22. What the Application Should Never Store

Never persist:

```text
Plain TOTP secrets
Plain backup passwords
Biometric templates
User passwords
Raw encryption keys
OTP history
Authentication logs containing secrets
```

Avoid unnecessary metadata as well.

For example, there is generally no reason for the application to maintain a history like:

```text
GitHub → OTP 382921 → 02:31
```

